# CSET (Cyber Security Evaluation Tool) Project Context

## Project Overview
CSET is a cybersecurity assessment tool developed by CISA and Idaho National Laboratory. It guides users through collecting facility-specific cybersecurity information and provides recommendations based on security standards and best practices.

## Architecture
- **Frontend**: Angular 19 (CSETWebNg/) - Single Page Application with Electron desktop support
- **Backend**: .NET 7+ Web API (CSETWebApi/) - RESTful API with multiple layers
- **Database**: Microsoft SQL Server 2022 (LocalDB for standalone, full SQL Server for enterprise)
- **Deployment**: Docker containers with Docker Compose orchestration

## Tech Stack

### Frontend (CSETWebNg/)
- Angular 19.2+ with TypeScript 5.8+
- Angular Material for UI components
- Bootstrap 5.3+ for styling
- Chart.js for data visualization
- Monaco Editor for code editing
- Electron for desktop app packaging
- SCSS for styling
- ESLint for code quality

### Backend (CSETWebApi/)
- .NET 7+ with C#
- ASP.NET Core Web API
- Entity Framework Core for data access
- SQL Server database
- RESTful API architecture
- Layered architecture pattern:
  - Controllers (API endpoints)
  - Business Logic layer
  - Data Access layer
  - Models and DTOs

### Database
- Microsoft SQL Server 2022
- Database scripts in DatabaseScripts/
- Entity Framework Code First approach

## Project Structure

### Frontend (CSETWebNg/)
```
src/
├── app/           # Angular application code
├── assets/        # Static assets
├── environments/  # Environment configurations
└── ...
```

### Backend (CSETWebApi/)
```
CSETWeb_Api/
├── CSETWebCore.ApiCore/        # Main API controllers
├── CSETWebCore.Business/       # Business logic layer
├── CSETWebCore.DataLayer/      # Data access layer
├── CSETWebCore.Model/          # Entity models
├── CSETWebCore.Interfaces/     # Interface definitions
└── ...
```

## Development Guidelines

### Code Style
- Follow C# naming conventions (PascalCase for public members, camelCase for private)
- Use Angular style guide for TypeScript/Angular code
- Use meaningful variable and method names
- Add XML documentation comments for public C# APIs
- Use TypeScript strict mode

### Security Considerations
- This is a cybersecurity tool - security is paramount
- Follow secure coding practices
- Validate all inputs
- Use parameterized queries
- Implement proper authentication and authorization
- Be mindful of sensitive data handling

### Testing
- Write unit tests for business logic
- Use Angular testing utilities for frontend tests
- Mock external dependencies in tests
- Maintain good test coverage

### Database
- Use Entity Framework Core for data access
- Database migrations should be in DatabaseScripts/
- Follow database naming conventions
- Always use parameterized queries

## Build and Deployment

### Local Development
- Use Docker Compose for full stack: `docker compose up`
- Frontend dev server: `cd CSETWebNg && npm start`
- API can be run from Visual Studio or `dotnet run`

### Environment Files
- Copy `env.dist` to `.env` for Docker configuration
- Contains database connection strings and other config

## Key Features to Understand
- Assessment creation and management
- Question sets and standards mapping
- Report generation and export
- Multi-user support
- Offline capability (standalone mode)
- Enterprise deployment options

## Important Notes
- This is open source government software (MIT/Apache 2.0 license)
- Maintained by CISA and Idaho National Laboratory
- Used for critical infrastructure cybersecurity assessments
- Must maintain high reliability and security standards

## Common Patterns
- Use dependency injection throughout the .NET stack
- Angular services for shared functionality
- RESTful API design principles
- Separation of concerns in layered architecture
- Repository pattern for data access 